{{#invoke:quote|call_template
|en
|author     = John Fryer
|authorlink = John Fryer (travel writer)
|chapter    = {{#if:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}|{{#if:{{R2A|{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}|no_error=1}}|The Contents|
  {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|II}}}}}=1 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}=10) or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<10|Treats of Embarking and Passage ’till past St. ''Iago''|
    {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=2 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>10) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<22|Contains Our Passage from St. ''Iago'' to ''Johanna'', and Relation of That Island|
      {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=3 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>22) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<26|Declares Our Course from ''Johanna'', to Our Landing at ''Mechlapatan''|
        {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>25 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<37|Takes up Our Stay at ''Mechlapatan'', to Our Landing at ''Fort St. George''|
          {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>36 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<50|Gives a True Narrative of the ''English'', ''French'' and ''Dutch'' on the Coast of ''Coromandel'', Continuing till We Double the Cape for the Coast of ''Malabar''|
            {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=6 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>50) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<61|Views the ''Malabar'' and ''Canatick'' Coasts up to ''Bombaim''|
              {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>60 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<61|Mentions the Island and Its Possessors|
                {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=2 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>70) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<73|Is a Survey of the Island of ''Canorein'' near ''Bombaim''|
                  {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=3 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>73) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<77|A Visit to ''Baçein'', a ''Portugal'' City in ''India''; Our Return by ''Elephanto'', and the ''Putachos'', with Observations on the Island ''Bombaim''|
                    {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=4 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>77) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<82|A Narrative of an Embassy to ''Seva Gi'', and Journey to ''Rairee'', the Court of the ''Rajah''|
                      {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=5 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>82) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<91|Our Passage to ''Swally'', and Relation of the ''English'' Presidency at ''Surat''|
                        {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>90 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<100|Animadversions on the City and People of ''Surat'' in the ''East-Indies''|
                          {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=2 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>100) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<107|Shews the Tombs, Outwalks, Ceremonies, and Austerities of the ''Gentiles'', with the Ships and River about ''Surat''|
                            {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=3 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>107) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<121|Of Their Solemnities, Sports and Pastimes; Their Marriages; of the ''Parseys'', Their Strength by Land and Sea, Their Abundant Wealth, and Fitness for Trade|
                              {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=4 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>121) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<131|Brings Me with a New Deputy-Governor from ''Surat'' to ''Bombaim'', and Sends me to the ''Mogul''’s General at ''Jeneah''|
                                {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>130 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<145|Introduces Me into the ''Nabob''’s Presence; My Business with Him; a Prospect of the ''Gur'' (''Seva Gi''’s Birth-Place); the Army and Country of ''Duccan'': Of the Pass of ''Tanaw'', and Benefit Thereof, Were It in the Hands of the ''English''|
                                  {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>144 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<149|Concerning Our Shipping for ''Carwar''; of the Factory There; the Unsetled Condition of the Place; and Our Coming to ''Goa''|
                                    {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=2 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>149) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<158|Takes a View of ''Goa''; Makes a Voyage to ''Vingula''; Engages with the ''Malabars'', and Returns Us to ''Carwar''|
                                      {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>157 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<165|A Pilgrimage to ''Gocurn'', Where Was a Great ''Gentile'' Solemnity. A Cursory Discourse of the Bordering Princes.|
                                        {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=4 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>165) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<175|Of the King and Kingdom of ''Visiapour''; Their Policy and Government; ''Hobsie-Cofferies'' Preferred: The Present Protector, and Other Members of ''Duccan'': The Life, Exploits, and Expeditions of ''Seva Gi'': The Wealth, Strength, and Powers of Each, with Respect to the Great ''Mogul'' as Supreme|
                                          {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=5 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>175) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<185|Shews the Pleasure and the Product of the Woods: The People Bewitched to Idolatry; the Sottishness of the Atheist. I am Sent for to ''Bombaim''; after Some Endeavours to Go Thither, and Some Time Spent at ''Goa'', Am Forced to Winter at ''Carwar'', and then I return to ''Surat''.|
                                            {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>184 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<202|A Special Chorography and History of ''East-India''. Chap. VI. Is a Summary Rehearsal of the Whole.|
                                              {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>201 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<217|Collections of the Coins, Weights, and Precious Stones, Usual in Those Places of Trade Within the Charter of the Honourable ''East-India''-Company|
                                                {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>216 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<230|Of Our crossing the Sea to the ''Persian'' Gulf, Arrival at ''Gombroon'', and Stay There|
                                                  {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=2 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>230) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<240|Our Setting Forth from ''Gombroon'', and leaving ''Lhor''|
                                                    {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>239 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<246|We Measure the Utmost Bounds of the Provinces of ''Lhor'' and ''Gerom'', to the City of ''Siras''|
                                                      {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=4 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>246) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<257|Of ''Siras'', and the Ancient City ''Persepolis'', and our Journying Quite Through ''Persia''|
                                                        {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=5 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>257) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<265|Our Progress into ''Parthia''; Entrance into ''Spahaun'', and Reception There|
                                                          {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=6 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>265) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<286|Carries Us to ''Jelfa''; Where Is Treated of the ''Gabers'', or Original ''Persians'', the ''Armenians'', ''Georgians'', and ''Europe'' Artisans|
                                                            {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=7 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>286) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<297|Gives Us a Sight of the Palace, and Rarities There; Our Entertainment by the ''French'' Artisans in the Emperor’s Service; the Diversions of the Place, and Its Product; and the Close of the Year, with Its Seasons|
                                                              {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=8 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>297) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<309|Brings Us in the Midst of Winter from ''Spahaun'' to ''Gombroon''; The ''Caun''’s Pranks There: The Hot Baths at ''Genoe'': The ''Equinox'' and Seasons Attending Are Somewhat Parallel Betwixt This Coast and the ''Indian'': A New Agent Arrives|
                                                                {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=9 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>309) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<320|We Go up in the Spring with our New Agent to ''Ispahaun'': Two ''Irish'' Greyhounds Sent for a Present to the Emperor: We Leave the Agent There, and Return in the Fall|
                                                                  {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>319 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<327|A Voyage to ''Congo'' for Pearl; a Discourse of Their Generation; Departure from ''Persia'', and Return to ''India''|
                                                                    {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>326 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<346|The Present State of ''Persia''. Chap. XI. Of the Various Names, Situation, and Bounds; the Temper of the Air; of the Seasons and Winds; of the High and Stupendious Mountains, Their Advantage and Conveniency; of the Fruitfulness of the Valleys, Occasioned by Snow upon the Hills: Of the Vegetables, Plants, and Minerals; of Their Fowl, Four-Footed Beasts, and Fishes: Their Caravans, Mosques, Hummums, Buzzars, Houses, and Bridges. The City ''Suffahaun'' Proposed as a Patern of Their Government.|
                                                                      {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>345 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<360|The Present State of ''Persia''. Chap. XII. Of the Present Inhabitants; of the ''Jews'' Being Interspersed Ever since the Captivity of ''Babylon''; the Custom of Exoculating Their Princes; Nobility among the ''Persians''; the Esteem They Have of the Emperor’s Person Being Divine; His Name and Succession: Of the ''English'' Overthrowing the ''Portugals'' at ''Ormus''; the Procession of the Court; Its Grandeur, and Reception of Ambassadors: Of the ''Suffees''; of Their Cavalry, Infantry, Seamen, and Navy.|
                                                                        {{#ifexpr:({{R2A|{{{chapter|I}}}}}=13 or {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>360) and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<388|The Present State of ''Persia''. Chap. XIII. Of Their Bookmen and Books; of Their Religion and Religious Worship; of Their Notions in Philosophy; of Heaven and Hell: Their Astronomers, Physicians, and Lawyers.|
                                                                          {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>387 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<408|The Present State of ''Persia''. Chap. XIV. The Appellations of the Warrior and the Scholar; of the Merchant, and Mechanicks and Villains; of Their Garb, Civility of Manners, Facetiousness, Revelling, Sports, Weddings, Cleansings and Burials; of Their Women, and License of Curtisans. The Eunuchs, Vertues and Vices; and Lastly, Their Supputations of Coin, Measures and Time.|
                                                                            {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>407 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<415|Takes Notice of ''Broach''; the Change of Governors at ''Surat''; Intestine Broils of the Empire; Rencounters of the ''English''; and Portentous Accidents of This Year|
                                                                              {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>414 and {{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}<420|Continues the General Occurrences with Remarks|
                                                                                {{#ifexpr:{{{page|{{{pageref|}}}}}}>419|Concludes with my Return to ''England''
                                                                                }}
