|chapter         = {{#if:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}
    | {{#switch:{{{part|{{{1|}}}}}}
        | I = {{#if:{{R2A|{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}|no_error=1}}
            | The Publisher to the Reader
            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<25
                | The Author Gives Some Account of Himself and Family, His First Inducements to Travel.{{nb...|He is Shipwrecked, and Swims for His Life, Gets Safe on Shoar in the Country of ''Lilliput'', is Made a Prisoner, and Carried up the Country.}}
                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<47
                    | The Emperor of ''Lilliput'', Attended by Several of the Nobility, Come to See the Author in His Confinement.{{nb...|The Emperor’s Person and Habit Describ’d. Learned Men Appointed to Teach the Author Their Language. He Gains Favour by His Mild Disposition. His Pockets are Searched, and His Sword and Pistols Taken from Him.}}
                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<65
                        | The Author Diverts the Emperor and His Nobility of Both Sexes in a Very Uncommon Manner.{{nb...|The Diversions of the Court of ''Lilliput'' Described. The Author has His Liberty Granted Him upon Certain Conditions.}}
                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<77
                            | ''Mildendo'', the Metropolis of ''Lilliput'', Described, together with the Emperor’s Palace.{{nb...|A Conversation between the Author and a Principal Secretary Concerning the Affairs of that Empire: The Author’s Offers to Serve the Emperor in His Wars.}}
                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<92
                                | The Author by an Extraordinary Stratagem Prevents an Invasion.{{nb...|A High Title of Honour is Conferred upon Him. Embassadors Arrive from the Emperor of ''Blefuscu'', and Sue for Peace. The Empress’s Apartment on Fire by an Accident; the Author Instrumental in Saving the Rest of the Palace.}}
                                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<115
                                    | Of the Inhabitants of ''Lilliput'';{{nb...|Their Learning, Laws, and Customs, the Manner of Educating Their Children. The Author’s Way of Living in that Country. His Vindication of a Great Lady.}}
                                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<134
                                        | The Author being Informed of a Design to Accuse Him of High-Treason, Makes His Escape to ''Blefuscu''.{{nb...|His Reception there.}}
                                        | The Author, by a Lucky Accident, Finds Means to Leave ''Blefuscu''; and, after Some Difficulties, Returns Safe to his Native Country.
                                      }}
                                  }}
                              }}
                          }}
                      }}
                  }}
              }}
          }}
        | II = {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<177
            | A Great Storm Described, the Long-Boat Sent to Fetch Water, the Author Goes with It to Discover the Country.{{nb...|He is Left on Shore, is Seized by One of the Natives, and Carried to a Farmer’s House. His Reception there, with Several Accidents that Happened there. A Description of the Inhabitants.}}
            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<191
                | A Description of the Farmer’s Daughter.{{nb...|The Author Carried to a Market-Town and then to the Metropolis. The Particulars of His Journey.}}
                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<215
                    | The Author Sent for to Court.{{nb...|The Queen Buys Him of His Master the Farmer, and Presents Him to the King. He Disputes with His Majesty’s Great Scholars. An Apartment at Court Provided for the Author. He is in High Favour with the Queen. He Stands up for the Honour of His Own Country. His Quarrels with the Queen’s Dwarf.}}
                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<226
                        | The Country Described.{{nb...|A Proposal for Correcting Modern Maps. The King’s Palace, and Some Account of the Metropolis. The Author’s Way of Travelling. The Chief Temple Described.}}
                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<249
                            | Several Adventures that Happened to the Author.{{nb...|The Execution of a Criminal. The Author Shews His Skill in Navigation.}}
                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<269
                                | Several Contrivances of the Author to Please the King and Queen.{{nb...|He Shews His Skill in Musick. The King Enquires into the State of ''Europe'', which the Author Relates to Him. The King’s Observations thereon.}}
                                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<284
                                    | The Author’s Love of His Country.{{nb...|He Makes a Proposal of Much Advantage to the King, which is Rejected. The King’s Great Ignorance in Politicks. The Learning of that Country Very Imperfect and Confined. Their Laws, and Military Affairs, and Parties in the State.}}
                                    | The King and Queen Make a Progress to the Frontiers. The Author Attends Them. The Manner in which He Leaves the Country Very Particularly Related. He Returns to ''England''.
                                  }}
                              }}
                          }}
                      }}
                  }}
              }}
          }}
        | III = {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<15
            | The Author Sets out on His Third Voyage; is taken by Pyrates.{{nb...|The Malice of a ''Dutchman''. His Arrival at an Island. He is Received into ''Laputa''.}}
            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<35
                | The Humours and Dispositions of the ''Laputians'' Described.{{nb...|An Account of Their Learning. Of the King and His Court. The Author’s Reception There. The Inhabitants Subject to Fears and Disquietudes. An Account of the Women.}}
                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<48
                    | A Phænomenon Solved by Modern Philosophy and Astronomy.{{nb...|The ''Laputians'' Great Improvements in the Latter. The King’s Method of Suppressing Insurrections.}}
                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<62
                        | The Author Leaves ''Laputa'', is Conveyed to ''Balnibarbi'', Arrives at the Metropolis.{{nb...|A Description of the Metropolis, and the Country Adjoining. The Author Hospitably Received by a Great Lord. His Conversation with that Lord.}}
                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<80
                            | The Author Permitted to See the Grand Academy of ''Lagado''.{{nb...|The Academy Largely Described. The Arts wherein the Professors Employ Themselves.}}
                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<94
                                | A Further Account of the Academy.{{nb...|The Author Proposes Some Improvements which are Honourably Received.}}
                                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<104
                                    | The Author Leaves ''Lagado'', Arrives at ''Maldonada''.{{nb...|No Ship Ready. He Takes a Short Voyage to ''Glubbdubdrib''. His Reception by the Governor.}}
                                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<118
                                        | A Further Account of ''Glubbdubdrib''.{{nb...|Antient and Modern History Corrected.}}
                                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<127
                                            | The Author’s Return to ''Maldonada''.{{nb...|Sails to the Kingdom of ''Luggnagg''. The Author Confined. He is Sent for to Court. The Manner of His Admittance. The King’s Great Lenity to His Subjects.}}
                                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<47
                                                | The ''Luggnaggians'' Commended.{{nb...|A Particular Description of the ''Struldbruggs'', with Many Conversations between the Author and Some Eminent Persons upon that Subject.}}
                                                | The Author Leaves ''Luggnagg'', and Sails to ''Japan''. From thence He Returns in a ''Dutch'' Ship to ''Amsterdam'', and from ''Amsterdam'' to ''England''.
                                              }}
                                          }}
                                      }}
                                  }}
                              }}
                          }}
                      }}
                  }}
              }}
          }}
        | IV = {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<172
            | The Author Sets out as Captain of a Ship.{{nb...|His Men Conspire against Him, Confine Him a Long Time to His Cabbin, Set Him on Shore in an Unknown Land. He Travels up in the Country. The ''Yahoos'' a Strange Sort of Animal Described. The Author Meets two ''Houyhnhnms''.}}
            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<187
                | The Author Conducted by a ''Houyhnhnm'' to His House.{{nb...|The House Described. The Author’s Reception. The Food of the ''Houyhnhnms''. The Author in Distress for Want of Meat, is at Last Relieved. His Manner of Feeding in this Country.}}
                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<201
                    | The Author Studious to Learn the Language, the ''Houyhnhnm'' His Master Assists in Teaching Him.{{nb...|The Language Described. Several ''Houyhnhnms'' of Quality Come out of Curiosity to See the Author. He Gives His Master a Short Account of His Voyage.}}
                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<214
                        | The ''Houyhnhnms'' Notion of Truth and Falshood.{{nb...|The Author’s Discourse Disapproved by His Master. The Author Gives a More Particular Account Himself, and the Accidents of His Voyage.}}
                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<234
                            | The Author at His Master’s Command Informs Him of the State of ''England''.{{nb...|The Causes of War among the Princes of ''Europe''. The Author Begins to Explain the ''English'' Constitution.}}
                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<252
                                | A Continuation of the State of ''England''; so Well Governed by a Queen as to Need no First Minister.{{nb...|The Character of Such an One in Some ''European'' Courts.}}
                                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<271
                                    | The Author’s Great Love of His Native Country.{{nb...|His Master’s Observations upon the Constitution and Administration of ''England'', as Described by the Author, with Parallel Cases and Comparisons. His Master’s Observations upon Human Nature.}}
                                    | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<286
                                        | The Author Relates Several Particulars of the ''Yahoos''.{{nb...|The Great Virtues of the ''Houyhnhnms''. The Education and Exercises of Their Youth. Their General Assembly.}}
                                        | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<299
                                            | A Grand Debate at the General Assembly of the ''Houyhnhnms'', and How It was Determined.{{nb...|The Learning of the ''Houyhnhnms''. Their Buildings. Their Manner of Burials. The Defectiveness of Their Language.}}
                                            | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<318
                                                | The Author’s Oeconomy and Happy Life among the ''Houyhnhnms''.{{nb...|His Great Improvement in Virtue, by Conversing with Them. Their Conversations. The Author has Notice Given Him by His Master that He Must Depart from the Country. He Falls into a Swoon for Grief, but Submits. He Contrives and Finishes a Canoo, by the Help of a Fellow-Servant, and Puts to Sea at a Venture.}}
                                                | {{#ifexpr:{{{page|}}}{{{pageref|}}}{{{2|}}}<338
                                                    | The Author’s Dangerous Voyage.{{nb...|He Arrives at ''New-Holland'', Hoping to Settle There. Is Wounded with an Arrow by One of the Natives. Is Seized and Carried by Force into a ''Portugueze'' Ship. The Great Civilities of the Captain. The Author Arrives at ''England''.}}
                                                    | The Author’s Veracity. His Design in Publishing this Work.{{nb...|His Censure of Those Travellers who Swerve from the Truth. The Author Clears Himself from any Sinister Ends in Writing. An Objection Answered. The Method of Planting Colonies. His Native Country Commended. The Right of the Crown to Those Countries Described by the Author is Justified. The Difficulty of Conquering Them. The Author Takes His Last Leave of the Reader: Proposeth His Manner of Living for the Future, Gives Good Advice, and Concludes.}}
                                                  }}
                                              }}
                                          }}
                                      }}
                                  }}
                              }}
                          }}
                      }}
