        | Abolishing of Christianity = [[w:An Argument Against Abolishing Christianity|An Argument to Prove that the Abolishing of Christianity in England, may as Things now Stand, be Attended with Some Inconveniencies, and Perhaps Not Produce Those Many Good Effects Proposed thereby]]
        | Accomplishment = The Accomplishment of the First of Mr. Bickerstaff’s Predictions. Being an Account of the Death of Mr. Partrige, the Almanack-maker. Upon the 29th Instant.{{nb...|In a Letter to a Person of Honour. Written in the Year, 1708.}}
        | Advancement of Religion = A Project for the Advancement of Religion, and the Reformation of Manners.{{nb...|By a Person of Quality. Written in the Year, 1709.}}
        | Apollo Outwitted = Apollo Outwitted.{{nb...|To the Honourable Mrs. Finch, under Her Name of Ardelia, Written, 1709.}}
        | Baucis and Philemon = [[w:Baucis and Philemon|Baucis and Philemon]]. Imitated, from the [[w:Metamorphoses|Eighth Book]] of [[w:Ovid|Ovid]].{{nb...|Written, 1706.}}
        | Biddy Floyd = To Mrs. Biddy Floyd.{{nb...|Anno. 1708.}}
        | Broom-stick = A Meditation upon a Broom-stick.{{nb...|According to the Style and Manner of the Honourable Robert Boyl’s Meditations. Written August, 1704.}}
        | City Shower = A Description of a City Shower.{{nb...|October, 1710.}}
        | Broom-stick|Broomstick = A Meditation upon a Broom-stick.{{nb...|According to the Style and Manner of the Honourable Robert Boyl’s Meditations. Written August, 1704.}}
        | Cutpurse = Lady B[etty] B[erkeley] Finding in the Authors Room Some Verses Unfinished, Underwrit a ''Stanza'' of Her Own, with Raillery upon Him, which Gave Occasion to This Ballade. {{...|August, 1702.}} To the Tune of the Cutpurse.
        | Drapier's Letter I = [[w:Drapier's Letters|A Letter to the Shop-keepers, Tradesmen, Farmers, and Common-people of Ireland, Concerning the Brass Half-pence Coined by one William Wood, Hard-ware-man, with a Design to have Them Pass in this Kingdom.{{nb...|Wherein is Shewn, the Power of His Patent, the Value of the Half-pence, and How Far Every Person may be Obliged to Take the Same in Payments, and How to Behave Himself, in Case Such an Attempt should be Made by Wood, or Any Other Person. (Very Proper to be Kept in Every Family.) By M. B. Drapier. Written in the Year 1724.}}]]
        | Faculties of the Mind = A Tritical Essay upon the Faculties of the Mind.{{nb...|August the 6th. 1707 [Julian calendar].}}
        | Harris = To Their Excellencies the Lords Justices of Ireland. The Humble Petition of ''Frances Harris'', Who Must Starve, and Die a Maid if It Miscarries.{{nb...|Anno. 1700.}}
        | History of Vanbrugh's House = The History of [[w:John Vanbrugh|V[anbrugh]]]’s House.{{nb...|Anno. 1708.}}
        | Lady's Ivory Table-book = Wrote in a Lady’s Ivory Table-book
        | Merlin = The Famous Prediction of Merlin, the British Wizard. Written above a Thousand Years ago, and Relating to this Present Year, 1709
        | Morning = A Description of the Morning.{{nb...|April, 1709.}}
        | Nobles and Commons = A Discourse of the Contests and Dissensions between the Nobles and the Commons in Athens and Rome, with the Consequences They Had upon Both Those States
        | Partridge Elegy = A Grubstreet Elegy on the Supposed Death of [[w:John Partridge (astrologer)|Patrige]] the Almanack-maker.{{nb...|Anno. 1708.}}
        | Predictions = Predictions for the Year 1708.{{nb...|Wherein the Month and Day of the Month are Set down, the Persons Named, and the Great Actions and Events of the Next Year Particularly Related, as They Will Come to Pass. Written to Prevent the People of England from being Farther Impos’d on by Vulgar Almanack-makers. By Isaac Bickerstaff Esq}}
        | Religion and Government = The Sentiments of a Church of England-man with Respect to Religion and Government
