:: Yes, you ''can'' block them. These are IPv6 IP addresses, which means each of those hexadecimal digits (0-F) is 4 bits, and each group of 4 digits (separated by ":", with leading "0"s often not shown) is 16 bits, for a total of 128 bits. All IP addresses start from the left, so everything with the lefthand side the same is in the same range. You specify a range by adding "/" followed by the number of bits that are the same.
:: In this case, you'll notice that all of the IP addresses start with "2001:14BA:16F0:A700". That's 4 groups of 4 hexadecimal digits, for a total of 64 bits in common (Internet service providers tend to assign addresses within a 64-bit range to a single user, so this is quite normal). If you add /64 to the end of the IP address, that selects everything that shares the same leading 64 bits. The lower the number, the larger the range, with /32 being the widest range the system will let you use.
:: Executive summary: [[Special:Contributions/2001:14BA:16F0:A700:D036:B783:6E7A:1956/64]] (or [[Special:Contributions/2001:14BA:16F0:A700:D903:4B7C:4BB4:FC33/64]], etc.) will display the contributions for all of the addresses above, and blocking 2001:14BA:16F0:A700:D036:B783:6E7A:1956/64, etc. will block all of them. I almost always block IPv6 IPs with /64. 
:: IPv4 addresses have only 4 groups of 8 bits (0-255) separated by ".", with /16 as the largest range the system allows.
:: As for communicating: they ''will'' see the block message, so you can tell them which talk page you want them to use in the block message (your own talk page would be the simplest).
:: I hope this helps. [[User:Chuck Entz|Chuck Entz]] ([[User talk:Chuck Entz|talk]]) 17:42, 7 January 2023 (UTC)
::: {{re|Chuck Entz}} Thanks for the explanation; I didn't know how to do that. I've blocked them now and in the block message asked them to message me here to discuss it. —[[User:Mahagaja|Mahāgaja]] · [[User talk:Mahagaja|''talk'']] 18:06, 7 January 2023 (UTC)
::::Hey! I received your block message and am sorry to have caused a nuisance with my sloppy edits. Too much copy-pasteing and not enough confirming with the sources. I will refrain from creating more of the complicated inflection tables from now on. 
::::Most of those incorrect inflection tables are the result of my copy-pasteing the inflection of a similar verb and then changing the parameters to suit without paying sufficient attention to checking for errors. Much of it was trial and error due to my poor understanding of how the inflection commands work. Is there some place in Wiktionary where you can see e.g. a list of commands? There seems to be a different set for every language.  
::::Is it incorrect to use commands such as {{tl|grc-conj-έω|}} 
::::to create a table and then fill it up, even if some of those forms might have no extant records?    
::::Also, I didn't know adding an inflection table to a non-lemma form was such a faux-pas. I found that adding one when filling e.g. an adjective inflection table made the work more efficient in regard to checking if I had overlooked something etc. Is there an article somewhere that lists the house rules?
::::Cheers and sorry for the hassle1! Didn't mean to appear elusive, my IP keeps changing for reasons unknown. I should probably get around to creating an account though. [[Special:Contributions/2001:14BA:16F0:A700:6D9C:6305:83DD:AA6D|2001:14BA:16F0:A700:6D9C:6305:83DD:AA6D]] 20:14, 7 January 2023 (UTC)
:::::Hi! First of all, yes, please ''do'' create an account. It will make life much easier for everyone, including yourself. It's true that the parameters for templates are different from language to language and sometimes even from template to template within a language. It can be confusing, but many templates have documentation explaining how to use them, and for the ones that don't, it's a good idea to see how they are currently being used on existing pages. (You can do that with the "Special:WhatLinksHere" function.) In my opinion, using templates like {{tl|grc-conj-έω}} and {{tl|grc-conj-ίζω}} is OK even if not all tenses are actually attested, but other editors may have a different opinion on that. Either way, though, you probably shouldn't create ''entries'' for inflected forms unless you're sure they're attested. As for tables on inflected forms, we do like to keep our nonlemma form entries as minimal as possible. I understand wanting to have the inflection table there so that you can double-check that everything is listed correctly – I do that myself – but you should check everything in the preview, and once you're satisfied, delete it again before hitting "Publish changes". Preview mode is also your friend for double-checking for mistakes and omissions – for example, in preview mode you can open the Pronunciation section and see if it's telling you to add any macrons or breves over vowels of ambiguous length. And just in general, edit more slowly. You often make dozens of edits in just a few minutes; slowing down and double-checking in Preview will make mistakes less likely. Go for quality rather than quantity. And please create an account so that people can contact you directly on your very own talk page! —[[User:Mahagaja|Mahāgaja]] · [[User talk:Mahagaja|''talk'']] 20:47, 7 January 2023 (UTC)

