== About the word pizza ==

[https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pizza pizza]
Sorry to bother you, but I have a question: What is the correct pronunciation of the word pizza? is it /ˈpiːt.sə/ or is it /ˈpɪt.sə/? From the audio, and pronunciations from other sources I cannot distinguish if it is iː or ɪ.<br>
Or... do both pronunciations are correct? [[User:FanNihongo|FanNihongo]] ([[User talk:FanNihongo|talk]]) 01:43, 12 January 2023 (UTC)
:[[pizza#Pronunciation]] says both pronunciations are correct, but I only use {{IPAchar|/iː/}} myself, and the audio file in that section also uses {{IPAchar|/iː/}}. Now, it is true that American English doesn't really have contrastive vowel length, and especially in that context (before a voiceless consonant cluster) the vowel is quite short, so a close phonetic transcription is {{IPAchar|[ˈpʰitsə]}}. But it's definitely the same vowel phoneme as in the word ''[[fleece]]'', not the vowel of the word ''[[kit]]''. Maybe some people do say it with the ''kit''-vowel, but I've never noticed that. —[[User:Mahagaja|Mahāgaja]] · [[User talk:Mahagaja|''talk'']] 08:16, 12 January 2023 (UTC)
::<small>Well, that's due to [//en.wiktionary.org/w/index.php?title=pizza&diff=66241020&oldid=66117512 diff]. But I too have only heard /i/; Cambridge, Collins, Dictionary.com, Longman, MacMillan, Merriam-Webster, Oxford Learner's and the first fifteen results as Youglish are all /i/.</small> <br/>FanNihongo, if you compare the 2nd through 7th Youglish results (i.e. all but the first result, where the speaker seems to be unsure of how to say the unusual name and produces /i/) of different people referring to the Australian [https://youglish.com/pronounce/Pitsa/english? ''Pitsa'' Binnion] /ˈpɪtsə/ and the results for [https://youglish.com/pronounce/pizza/english? ''pizza''] /ˈpi(ː)tsə/, they show the difference between /ˈpi(ː)tsə/ and /ˈpɪtsə/ fairly well, I think. (Even the [https://youglish.com/pronounce/pizza/english/aus? Australian pronunciations of ''pizza''] are distinct from ''Pitsa'', despite Australians tending to produce the {{small|FLEECE}} and {{small|KIT}} vowels very close together.) [[User:-sche|- -sche]] [[User talk:-sche|(discuss)]] 19:16, 12 January 2023 (UTC)
::{{re|-sche}} The entry says that South African English has a variant spelling {{m|en|pitza}}; I wonder whether that might be pronounced with {{smallcaps|kit}}. After 25 years of living in Germany, {{IPAchar|/ˈpɪtsə/}} doesn't "feel wrong" to me anymore, though I still wouldn't use it myself. —[[User:Mahagaja|Mahāgaja]] · [[User talk:Mahagaja|''talk'']] 19:56, 12 January 2023 (UTC)
:::Thank you very much for your answers. [[User:FanNihongo|FanNihongo]] ([[User talk:FanNihongo|talk]]) 04:23, 13 January 2023 (UTC)
